  "ExplanationCorrect": "Make-vs-buy decisions involve both quantitative and qualitative dimensions. Quantitative: Relevant cost to make = ($25 × 20,000) + $40,000 avoidable fixed = $540,000. Relevant cost to buy = ($22 × 20,000) − $50,000 sublease = $440,000 − $50,000 = $390,000. Quantitative advantage to outsourcing = $540,000 − $390,000 = $150,000. Qualitative: A 3.2/5 quality rating means approximately 64% satisfaction — below typical acceptance thresholds. A 12% missed delivery rate means approximately 2,400 units per year could arrive late, potentially halting production lines. At $25 per unit in-house cost, even a 15% effective cost increase from quality/delivery issues (inspection, rework, production stoppages, customer penalties) would add approximately $66,000, partially offsetting the $150,000 savings. The correct approach is to quantify the expected cost of supplier failures, add it to the purchase price, and compare the total expected cost against in-house production — not to dismiss the supplier based on qualitative factors alone or to accept the bid ignoring those factors.",
