  "ExplanationCorrect": "Under CVP analysis, the sales dollars required to earn a target profit are computed by adding the target profit to fixed costs and dividing the sum by the contribution margin ratio: ($150,000 + $50,000) ÷ 0.40 = $200,000 ÷ 0.40 = $500,000. Summit Apparel must generate $500,000 of sales to earn its $50,000 target profit. The reasoning: each sales dollar contributes 40 cents toward fixed costs and profit, so $200,000 of required contribution demands $500,000 of revenue. A common error is grossing up only the fixed costs (which yields the breakeven point of $375,000) or only the profit ($125,000) — the numerator must contain BOTH amounts.",
  "ExplanationWrongA": "$375,000 is the breakeven point: $150,000 ÷ 0.40 = $375,000. This covers fixed costs only and leaves no room for the $50,000 target profit. A candidate reaching this answer has correctly applied the CM ratio but omitted the target profit from the numerator. Both amounts must be grossed up together: ($150,000 + $50,000) ÷ 0.40 = $500,000.",
  "ExplanationWrongB": "",
  "ExplanationWrongC": "$125,000 results from grossing up only the target profit: $50,000 ÷ 0.40 = $125,000. This treats the contribution margin as though it needed to cover nothing but profit, ignoring the $150,000 of fixed costs that must be recovered first. The required contribution is the SUM of fixed costs and target profit, $200,000, which at a 40% CM ratio requires $500,000 of sales.",
  "ExplanationWrongD": "$550,000 arises from double-counting the target profit. After correctly grossing up ($150,000 + $50,000) ÷ 0.40 = $500,000, a candidate who adds the $50,000 target profit a second time reaches $550,000. The $50,000 is already embedded in the $500,000; adding it again overstates the required sales by exactly that amount.",
  "FormulaReference": "DA-05: Target Profit (sales $) = (Fixed Costs + Target Profit) / CM Ratio",
