  "ExplanationCorrect": "A profit-maximizing price-setter produces where marginal revenue equals marginal cost. With linear demand P = 60 − 0.005Q, marginal revenue has twice the slope: MR = 60 − 0.01Q. Setting MR = MC: 60 − 0.01Q = 20 → 0.01Q = 40 → Q = 4,000 units. At 4,000 units the price is P = 60 − 0.005(4,000) = $40. Valentis earns its maximum profit because each unit up to 4,000 adds more to revenue than its $20 cost, and each unit beyond 4,000 adds less than $20. A common error is setting price equal to marginal cost (P = MC, giving 8,000 units) — that rule applies to price-TAKING firms, not to a firm facing a downward-sloping demand curve where each extra unit also lowers the price on all units sold.",
