  "ExplanationCorrect": "Under pricing theory, price discrimination succeeds only when three conditions hold. First, the segments must have DIFFERENT price elasticities of demand — the firm charges more where demand is inelastic and less where it is elastic. Second, the segments must be separable, meaning customers cannot resell the service between groups at a profit. Third, the firm must possess some market power; a price taker cannot charge different prices because competitors undercut it. When these conditions hold, segmentation converts consumer surplus into revenue for Apex Transit. Nothing in the conditions requires costs to differ across segments — the pricing reflects willingness to pay, not cost. A common error is assuming any price difference is illegal; lawful price discrimination is routine in logistics, airlines, and software.",
  "ExplanationWrongA": "Costs are not among the conditions. Apex Transit could serve two segments at identical cost and still profit from charging different prices, because the differences are driven by demand elasticity and willingness to pay, not cost. The candidate is importing a cost-based pricing mindset into a demand-based pricing model.",
