  "ExplanationCorrect": "The abandonment decision must be decomposed into its incremental effects. First, Line Z's own contribution to company profit: $400,000 − $300,000 − $60,000 = +$40,000, which dropping forfeits. Second, the cannibalization offset: dropping shifts 20% × $400,000 = $80,000 of sales to Line Y, which earns a 25% contribution margin ratio, recovering 25% × $80,000 = +$20,000 of the lost contribution. Net change = −$40,000 + $20,000 = −$20,000. BlueWave is $20,000 WORSE off if it drops Line Z, so the line should be kept. The analysis breaks the decision into segment contribution and demand shift before combining them. A common error is stopping at the $40,000 loss (ignoring the $20,000 recapture) or the $60,000 fixed-cost saving (ignoring both the lost contribution margin and the shift).",
  "ExplanationWrongA": "The $40,000 is contribution FORFEITED when Line Z is dropped, not saved — it is profit given up. Dropping eliminates a +$40,000 net contribution; the only genuine recovery is the $20,000 of contribution margin from sales shifted to Line Y. The candidate has mistaken a lost benefit for a saving.",
