  "ExplanationCorrect": "Under pricing strategy theory, bundling converts a distribution of reservation prices into one price that captures more consumer surplus. It is most powerful when reservation prices are NEGATIVELY correlated — one customer values the spreadsheet highly and the database little, while another has the opposite pattern. Selling separately at high prices earns little; a bundle price near the sum of the two moderate valuations captures both customers' surplus. Nimbus chooses between pure bundling (only the bundle is sold) and mixed bundling (the bundle plus individual products), whichever extracts more value. A common error is assuming bundling always works — with positively correlated valuations it adds little, and a bad bundle price can reduce revenue below separate pricing.",
