  "CorrectChoice": "A",
  "Difficulty": "Difficult",
  "DifficultyScore": 4,
  "ExplanationCorrect": "The newsvendor model orders the quantity that balances the cost of understocking against the cost of overstocking. Cost of underage Cu = $75 − $35 = $40 (profit lost on each unit of demand that cannot be served). Cost of overage Co = $35 − $20 = $15 (loss on each unit that must be salvaged). The critical ratio is Cu ÷ (Cu + Co) = $40 ÷ $55 = 0.727 — the optimal service level. With normal demand μ = 500 and σ = 100, the z-score corresponding to 0.727 is approximately 0.60. Order quantity = μ + zσ = 500 + 0.60(100) = 560 units. FreshFarm should order 560 units. A common error is ordering the mean (ignoring the asymmetry — underage is far more costly than overage) or plugging the ratio itself in as the z-score.",
  "ExplanationWrongA": "",
  "ExplanationWrongB": "500 units is the mean, which implies a 0.5 service level — optimal only when underage and overage costs are equal. Here underage ($40) outweighs overage ($15), so the optimal service level is 0.727 and FreshFarm should stock ABOVE the mean. Ordering the mean systematically understocks when the margin is fat relative to the salvage loss.",
  "ExplanationWrongC": "600 units corresponds to a z-score of 1.0, which implies a service level of about 0.84. The critical ratio is 40 ÷ 55 = 0.727, whose z-score is approximately 0.60. Overordering to 600 raises overage costs ($15 per excess unit) beyond the optimal trade-off.",
  "ExplanationWrongD": "527 units arises from using Co ÷ (Cu + Co) = 15 ÷ 55 = 0.273 as the z-score: 500 + 0.273(100) ≈ 527. The formula puts the UNDERAGE cost in the numerator: 40 ÷ 55 = 0.727. The inversion flips the service level and under-orders by about 33 units.",
  "FormulaReference": "Newsvendor: Critical Ratio = Cu / (Cu + Co); Q = μ + zσ",
