  "ExplanationCorrect": "Cash breakeven excludes non-cash charges because the company only needs cash to cover cash outflows. Cash fixed costs = Total Fixed Costs − Depreciation = $900,000 − $150,000 = $750,000. Contribution margin per unit = $60 − $24 = $36. Cash breakeven = $750,000 / $36 = 20,833.3 units, which must be rounded UP to 20,834 whole units to fully cover cash costs. The accounting breakeven of 25,000 units ($900,000 / $36) is higher because depreciation is a book entry, not a cash payment. For Ridgeway's treasury planning, 20,834 units is the monthly volume at which cash outflows are covered even though the income statement still shows a loss equal to the depreciation charge.",
  "ExplanationWrongA": "25,000 units is the ACCOUNTING breakeven ($900,000 / $36). It includes $150,000 of depreciation in the numerator, but depreciation requires no cash outflow. A candidate selecting this has treated the income-statement breakeven and the cash breakeven as the same number. The cash breakeven removes depreciation and is therefore lower.",
