  "ExplanationCorrect": "Multi-product breakeven uses the weighted-average contribution margin based on the sales mix. Standard: $100 − $60 = $40 CM. Premium: $180 − $100 = $80 CM. New mix 60/40: WACM = (0.60 × $40) + (0.40 × $80) = $24 + $32 = $56. Breakeven units = $540,000 / $56 = 9,642.9, rounded up to 9,643 units (approximately 5,786 Standard and 3,857 Premium). The old 70/30 mix would have produced WACM = (0.70 × $40) + (0.30 × $80) = $52 and a breakeven of 10,385 units — the shift toward the higher-margin Premium product lowers the breakeven volume.",
  "ExplanationWrongA": "10,385 units is the breakeven under the OLD 70/30 mix: WACM = (0.70 × $40) + (0.30 × $80) = $52, and $540,000 / $52 = 10,384.6. The question asks for the breakeven AFTER the mix shifts to 60/40, so the weighted average must be recomputed with the new weights.",
  "ExplanationWrongB": "9,000 units results from averaging the two contribution margins without weights: ($40 + $80) / 2 = $60, then $540,000 / $60 = 9,000. An unweighted average is correct only when both products carry the same mix percentage. With a 60/40 mix, the weighted average of $56 is the correct divisor.",
  "ExplanationWrongC": "",
