  "ExplanationCorrect": "Value-based pricing starts from the value the customer perceives, not from cost. The customer survey shows $210 of perceived value; the cost floor is $140 and the competitor charges $180. A price of $195 captures nearly all of the perceived value while undercutting the competitor — it also leaves a $55 margin per unit, far above the $14 margin of a 10% cost-plus price. Cost-based approaches like $140 or $154 leave value on the table, and $225 exceeds the customer's willingness to pay. Value-based pricing is appropriate here because customer economics (lifetime savings), not production cost, determine what the product is worth.",
