  "CommonTrapReference": "Applying the learning-curve average time to the repeat batch alone — the incremental hours for the SECOND batch equal the difference between cumulative total hours and the first batch's hours.",
  "CorrectChoice": "C",
  "Difficulty": "Difficult",
  "DifficultyScore": 4,
  "ExplanationCorrect": "Under the 80% cumulative average-time model, doubling cumulative output reduces the average time per unit to 80% of its prior level. First batch: 500 units took 2,000 hours, so the average is 4.0 hours per unit. At a cumulative 1,000 units, the average falls to 4.0 × 80% = 3.2 hours, and cumulative total hours = 3.2 × 1,000 = 3,200. Incremental hours for the second batch = 3,200 − 2,000 = 1,200 hours. Labor cost = 1,200 × $30 = $36,000; materials = 500 × $25 = $12,500; total cost = $48,500; price with a 20% markup = $48,500 × 1.20 = $58,200, or $116.40 per unit. Quoting below this price would earn less than the 20% target on the repeat order.",
  "ExplanationWrongA": "$174.00 assumes the second batch takes the same 2,000 hours as the first (no learning): (2,000 × $30 + $12,500) × 1.20 = $87,000, or $174.00 per unit. The 80% learning curve means the second batch needs only 1,200 incremental hours, so this price overstates cost and forfeits the order.",
  "ExplanationWrongB": "$145.20 applies the 3.2-hour cumulative average to the 500-unit batch alone: (3.2 × 500 × $30 + $12,500) × 1.20 = $72,600, or $145.20 per unit. That double-counts learning already earned in the first batch. The correct incremental hours are 3,200 − 2,000 = 1,200, not 1,600.",
  "ExplanationWrongC": "",
  "ExplanationWrongD": "$97.00 is cost without markup: $48,500 / 500 units. The customer request requires a 20% markup, so the price must be $116.40 per unit. Omitting the markup leaves the target margin entirely unearned.",
