  "CorrectChoice": "B",
  "Difficulty": "Moderate",
  "DifficultyScore": 3,
  "ExplanationCorrect": "The shadow price of a constraint is the change in the objective (contribution margin) from relaxing that constraint by one unit — but only at the optimum and only if the constraint is binding. The machine-hour constraint (3X + 1.5Y ≤ 600) is binding at the optimum (X = 50, Y = 300), while the demand caps for both products are not. An additional machine hour is used to produce one-third more of X (X needs 3 hours), adding $45 / 3 = $15 of contribution. Since Y's demand cap is exhausted, extra hours cannot produce more Y. The shadow price is therefore $15 per machine hour — the maximum rational payment for one additional hour of bottleneck capacity.",
  "ExplanationWrongA": "$45 is X's contribution margin per UNIT. One additional machine hour produces only one-third of a unit of X, worth $15. Quoting the unit margin as the hourly value overstates the capacity's worth threefold.",
  "ExplanationWrongB": "",
